{"connection_id": "BC-b66f34c797c769f3049c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "belief without seeing", "note": "The commentary says its text does not establish that belief follows sight; Peter says his readers love and believe in Christ though they have not seen him. This is another direct Christian formulation of faith without sight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-caae248b71120c581d0e", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Pet.1.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "belief without sight", "note": "The commentary's visual route leads to assent; Peter addresses people who love and believe Christ although they have not seen him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11a76595bf1a4d99f6c7", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.14", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "admonish, encourage, and be patient", "note": "The commentary's final pair is reciprocal counsel and patience; Paul tells the community to admonish the disorderly, encourage the fainthearted, help the weak, and be patient with all. The verse combines distinct forms of counsel with sabr-like endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-692ed51dadfdb711bb57", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.21", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "test everything, retain the good", "note": "The commentary describes confirming an affair and finding it sound; Paul tells the community to test everything and hold fast to what is good. The language directly joins testing with discernment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a11a53971bd867c1716c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Thess.5.21", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "test and retain", "note": "The commentary makes believing an act of checking and holding to what proves true; Paul tells the community to test everything and hold fast the good."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3bc9996d2118810af61b", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.13", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Psalm 116 read as faith then speech", "note": "The commentary carries truth from belief into testimony; Paul uses the Greek wording of Psalm 116:10 to say that he believes and therefore speaks of resurrection, a causal reading sharper than the Hebrew verse itself requires."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e462a54b3b1eb5504d69", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unbelief as blindness", "note": "The commentary permits loss to be read as blindness; Paul says the god of this age has blinded unbelievers' minds so they cannot see the gospel's light."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bbb384d4b6b447ede2ce", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.4.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "light yields knowledge", "note": "The commentary moves from seeing to certainty about truth; Paul says God's light shines in hearts to give knowledge of God's glory in Christ's face."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b2dca463eac21811939", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.7", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "walking by faith, not sight", "note": "The commentary's proposed reading moves from sight to confirmation but marks it as unproven; Paul says believers walk by faith rather than sight. This preserves a direct contrary formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-13eb1480ebc5644f0296", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Cor.5.7", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith beyond sight", "note": "The commentary's visual sequence is only one path to faith; Paul says his community walks by faith rather than sight while away from the Lord."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1822a99a119ec2b7e0d1", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.10", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "confirm by doing", "note": "The commentary proceeds from faith and virtue toward certainty; Peter tells believers to be diligent to confirm their calling through the conduct just listed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c1b8d4de5ead5904dc3", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyewitnesses against invented tale", "note": "The commentary asks whether a transmitted account rests on perception; Peter rejects clever myths and appeals to eyewitnesses of Christ's majesty."}
{"connection_id": "BC-023d21d3242c9e3f8048", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Pet.1.9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "moral failure as poor sight", "note": "The commentary's far-looking working eye stands for discernment; this letter calls one who lacks the listed virtues blind and nearsighted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74ab0034fcf3369f6e6a", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.2.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unbelief in truth and unrighteousness", "note": "The commentary's exception joins belief, truth, and righteous conduct against loss; Paul speaks of judgment on those who did not believe truth but took pleasure in unrighteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e40e3287be66c3754667", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.3.13", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do not tire of doing good", "note": "The commentary pairs righteous action with patience; Paul tells the community not to grow weary in doing good. It directly joins sustained action with endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-04a88c6d17f611ef6af3", "ref": "SBLGNT:2Thess.3.15", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "admonish as a brother", "note": "The commentary's tawasaw is mutual counsel; Paul tells the community to admonish an offender as a brother, not treat him as an enemy. The verse supplies a concrete relational form for correction."}
{"connection_id": "BC-78b162d058bc8d189f48", "ref": "SBLGNT:3John.1.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "testimony confirmed by a life", "note": "The commentary links transmitted truth and right deeds; brothers testify to Gaius's truth just as he walks in truth, letting conduct corroborate their report."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5bacdcde3cb5a1340e5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.12.15", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "report of Peter rejected", "note": "The commentary's hearers must judge a report before accepting it; the praying group tells Rhoda she is out of her mind when she reports that Peter is at the door."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a21ba5f444832a1e58d6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.12.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sight confirms disputed report", "note": "The commentary moves from relayed news to seeing for oneself; when Peter keeps knocking, the group opens the door, sees him, and is astonished."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b9b7e453f1eb7f4eecad", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "receive and examine", "note": "The commentary links hearing with confirmation; the Bereans receive Paul's message eagerly and examine the scriptures daily to see whether his claims are so. The scene models welcome joined to verification."}
{"connection_id": "BC-12a88eb0039dbbaddc57", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.17.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "examine message daily", "note": "The commentary joins receiving a report to active verification; the Bereans receive Paul's word eagerly and examine the Scriptures daily to see whether it is so."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3be17ee7922cac0b50e7", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.18.26", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "received teaching corrected", "note": "The commentary's truth is conveyed between people after examination; Priscilla and Aquila hear Apollos and explain God's way to him more accurately."}
{"connection_id": "BC-81dc52b745565929306e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.26.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vision appoints a witness", "note": "The commentary's observer must pass on what was seen; Paul says the risen Jesus appeared to appoint him a witness to what he had seen and what would yet be shown."}
{"connection_id": "BC-269982eddbca92ead9be", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.26.18", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes opened toward faith", "note": "The commentary permits loss as blindness and belief as sight; Paul's commission is to open eyes and turn people from darkness to light so they receive a share among those sanctified by faith."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d26b93cf36fd4f37efe", "ref": "SBLGNT:Acts.28.26", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah's hearing formula applied by Paul", "note": "The commentary leaves sensory failure as an unproven reading; Paul quotes Isaiah's command to hear without understanding and see without perceiving when addressing his Roman hearers. This is a later Christian application of the same formula."}

