{"connection_id": "BC-9740200a52a7e9a6018e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.22", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "doers, not hearers alone", "note": "The commentary moves from hearing or seeing to righteous action; James commands readers to be doers of the word, not hearers only who deceive themselves. It makes perception without action a self-deception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-00ea2a49c72a5646caa4", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.23", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a face seen in a mirror", "note": "The commentary cites the eye's small visible likeness in its pupil; James compares a hearer who does not act to someone looking at their face in a mirror. The image links seeing oneself with hearing a word."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4744b07485e3a428883f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.23", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "self seen in mirror", "note": "The commentary's small figure in the eye makes sight reflexive; James likens a hearer who does not act to someone looking at his own face in a mirror."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4df086dd0f252417c9e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.24", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing and then forgetting", "note": "The commentary develops a tiny image seen and then a process of confirmation; James says the mirror-gazer leaves and forgets what he saw. The verse gives a specific failure to retain what perception disclosed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d79f892bb1fbcff39e8e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.24", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sight forgotten without action", "note": "The commentary makes visual recognition part of a path to deeds; James' mirror viewer goes away and immediately forgets what he saw, explaining why the hearer who does not act fails."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a5cca141c87eb449728e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.25", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inspection becomes doing", "note": "The commentary joins mental inspection to righteous action; James says one who looks into the law and persists as a doer is blessed in the doing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-da6b8835deac1c8a37ea", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith tested, endurance produced", "note": "The commentary's faith and patience stand together; James says the testing of faith produces endurance. It supplies a causal link between belief and sabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ad864cde12939b76f56b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.1.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "endurance made complete", "note": "The commentary closes with counsel to patience; James says endurance must complete its work so believers may be mature. It treats patience as an active process rather than passive delay."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2de79eef7b663be23409", "ref": "SBLGNT:Jas.2.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith without works", "note": "The commentary's exception names both belief and righteous deeds; James says faith without works is dead. The verse makes action a condition of living faith."}
