{"connection_id": "BC-a30bef4344ff48289be8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.2", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyewitnesses transmit", "note": "The commentary moves from firsthand perception to handing on truth; Luke names eyewitnesses and ministers of the word as those who delivered the account later investigated by him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4edeb388890859882555", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "careful inquiry before writing", "note": "The commentary's mental work tests a report before transmission; Luke says he has followed everything carefully from the beginning before composing an orderly account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7254c63359fc4dd82fde", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "certainty about what was taught", "note": "The commentary glosses haqq as verifying an affair and becoming certain; Luke says he writes so Theophilus may know the certainty of what he was taught. This is an explicit account of checked transmission."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7790250245a275b8bf16", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.1.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "certainty about instruction", "note": "The commentary ends in assurance about what was conveyed; Luke writes so Theophilus may know the certainty of the things he was taught."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1751b7e22970ed1ce2ab", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "a report first disbelieved", "note": "The commentary cites a report that a listener might reject; the apostles treat the women's account of the empty tomb as nonsense and do not believe them. This is a Gospel scene of a true report meeting disbelief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16463c9f7280688e149e", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "report initially disbelieved", "note": "The commentary treats a report as something hearers may refuse pending confirmation; the apostles regard the women's resurrection account as nonsense and do not believe them."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dc93aa3b5ea390a00863", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "inspection after the report", "note": "The commentary connects verification with seeing what is there; Peter runs to the tomb, sees the linen cloths, and marvels after the women's report. The verse adds physical inspection to the disputed testimony."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0d9884995553ea51baaf", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes blocked from recognition", "note": "The commentary's process requires noticing what is before one; the Emmaus disciples' eyes are kept from recognizing Jesus even as they walk with him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-07a7b3461a7212d10322", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes opened in recognition", "note": "The commentary's progression culminates in recognizing what is truly present; the disciples' eyes are opened and they recognize the risen Jesus."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ed1cab9784f89566bfa", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.32", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words kindle understanding", "note": "The commentary combines received words with working the mind; the Emmaus disciples recall their hearts burning as Jesus opened the Scriptures on the road."}

