{"connection_id": "BC-7e90de7f1ec5c1035822", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.39", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "see and touch to verify", "note": "The commentary seeks certainty beyond a received report; the risen Jesus invites the disciples to look at his hands and feet and touch him to verify that he has a body."}
{"connection_id": "BC-226211e1442866ef8180", "ref": "SBLGNT:Luke.24.45", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "minds opened to understand", "note": "The commentary explicitly names exerting the mind after seeing and hearing; Jesus opens the disciples' minds to understand the Scriptures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e521c8cdceade960d82b", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.13", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "parables and failed perception", "note": "The commentary says blindness is not established by its plain wording; Jesus explains that he speaks in parables because people see without seeing and hear without hearing. This Christian interpretation makes the sensory failure explicit."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ae524622b69c1537d2cd", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.13", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing and hearing without understanding", "note": "The commentary reads loss as failure to perceive despite eyes and ears; Jesus explains parables by saying people see without seeing and hear without understanding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0735e3aa0607f8760b26", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.14", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Isaiah applied to hearers", "note": "The commentary leaves the blindness reading as an interpretive possibility; Matthew says Isaiah's prophecy is fulfilled in hearers who do not understand. It records a Christian application of the prophetic sensory formula."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c4d1710d1a523663812c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.15", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "closed eyes and possible healing", "note": "The commentary contrasts loss with a possible process of seeing and testing; Matthew's quotation says closed eyes prevent seeing, understanding, turning, and being healed. It gives that reading a concrete Christian interpretive context."}
{"connection_id": "BC-31982a6ce8aabd3dc2c8", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.13.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "blessed eyes that see", "note": "The commentary allows the exception to be read as seeing, while noting the surah does not prove it; Jesus calls the disciples' seeing eyes and hearing ears blessed. This is an explicit positive counterpart to failed perception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-27879fb7a2a27dba1366", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.28.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "vision with lingering doubt", "note": "The commentary's seeing does not automatically yield assurance; disciples see the risen Jesus and worship him, yet some doubt."}
{"connection_id": "BC-702e875c897ced64acb5", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fruit tests a prophet", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with righteous deeds and treats truth as something confirmed; Jesus says false prophets are recognized by their fruits. Conduct becomes a test of a religious claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73b47d54208a465392ff", "ref": "SBLGNT:Matt.7.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "known by fruits", "note": "The commentary lets observed acts test what a person claims; Jesus says false prophets are recognized by their fruits."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dff0d33f52633c4d5b85", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.1.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seen vision sent to churches", "note": "The commentary ends with conveying what one has seen; the voice tells John to write his vision and send it to seven churches."}
{"connection_id": "BC-652961146ba31e828794", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false assurance is blindness", "note": "The commentary reads human loss as failure to see oneself rightly; the Laodicean speaker claims wealth and needlessness without knowing he is wretched, poor, and blind."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f17e0eb5b1e63df54a5c", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rev.3.18", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eye salve to see", "note": "The commentary makes seeing a corrective to mistaken assurance; the risen Christ counsels Laodicea to obtain salve for its eyes so it may see its condition."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a6220bd6150f80f9c0f", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.10.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith comes through hearing", "note": "The commentary notes that perception includes hearing and glosses iman as assent; Paul says faith comes from hearing the message. This gives the hearing-to-belief sequence a direct Christian formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6138f7f39b6d03a9e71", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.10.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "faith comes through hearing", "note": "The commentary begins with auditory perception before assent; Paul says faith comes from hearing the message of Christ, making the received word a route to belief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fdaa43b6f0a9c8ad0c06", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.2.13", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "doers rather than hearers", "note": "The commentary begins with seeing or hearing and then names righteous action; Paul says hearers of the law are not righteous, but doers will be justified. It makes response in action decisive."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b664f35cd4a546e2ecc", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Paul reads Genesis 15:6", "note": "The commentary pairs iman with deeds; Paul cites Abraham's belief being counted as righteousness. This Christian reading foregrounds credited belief in the same Abraham text."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ba9d9960d98403bcae1", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.4.5", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "belief apart from a worker's wage", "note": "The commentary's exception explicitly combines belief and deeds; Paul contrasts the worker's wage with the person who believes in the God who justifies the ungodly. This interpretation places emphasis on belief apart from earned payment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9220c0cf8dbe3add667d", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "suffering produces endurance", "note": "The commentary couples patience with the path out of loss; Paul says suffering produces endurance. This Christian formulation gives hardship a formative relation to sabr."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8f6618faf4e8da2bd811", "ref": "SBLGNT:Rom.5.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "tested character and hope", "note": "The commentary's haqq includes confirmation and certainty; Paul says endurance produces tested character, which produces hope. The progression makes endurance itself a proving process."}
{"connection_id": "BC-46458ae81abf1b0462f6", "ref": "SBLGNT:Titus.1.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "deeds refute claimed knowledge", "note": "The commentary demands faith joined to good works; Titus says some profess to know God but deny him by their works."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5230472112d8ad1f57a6", "ref": "1QS 5:24-25", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual reproof in truth and humility", "note": "The commentary reads tawasaw bil-haqq as people transmitting confirmed truth to one another; the Community Rule directs members to reprove one another in truth and humility. It makes reciprocal correction a discipline of the community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3e1395502acf18c47f1d", "ref": "Babylonian Talmud, Makkot 24a", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Habakkuk's faithfulness as a summary", "note": "The commentary keeps belief, deeds, truth, and patience together; Makkot's list of prophetic summaries ends with Habakkuk's righteous person living by faithfulness. The rabbis make that one principle a compression of the commandments, a different interpretive emphasis."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d8b453ed35e0527ecdc4", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 56:1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Abraham sees a cloud on the mountain", "note": "The commentary develops distant sight as perception that can lead to certainty; this midrash reads Abraham's sight of the mountain as seeing a cloud of divine presence, distinguishing his perception from the servants'. It turns seeing into recognition of a sign."}
{"connection_id": "BC-40b95dab741815c37604", "ref": "Genesis Rabbah 84:21", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "Jacob cannot be comforted for the living", "note": "The commentary's Jacob rejects the brothers' death report and speaks of patience; this midrash explains his refusal of comfort in Genesis 37:35 by saying consolation for the dead does not take hold when the person is still alive, making the refusal a possible clue to Joseph's survival."}
{"connection_id": "BC-14a721ef3fb247d340c1", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "doing compared with study", "note": "The commentary's exception requires righteous deeds as well as belief; Avot says action is the main thing rather than exposition. The rabbinic maxim gives practice priority in a distinct, concise formulation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb9e3983c3a0f7fd70f4", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 1:17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "action over study", "note": "The commentary puts thinking and right action in sequence; this saying prizes doing above study, sharpening the point that mental work must become conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1ca6c13f968aa7e05a2", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 3:9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "deeds exceed wisdom", "note": "The commentary places mental work beside righteous deeds; this saying holds that wisdom endures when a person's deeds exceed it, making practice a test of learned insight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-01a9734c202c83cbbb51", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 5:21", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "ages of responsibility", "note": "The commentary's example of orphan property makes observed sound judgment the trigger for handing over wealth; Avot assigns stages of life to commandments, study, and livelihood. The rabbinic age ladder offers a specific, though not identical, account of recognized maturity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c05f4fb196e13e10a08c", "ref": "Mishnah Sanhedrin 5:1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seven interrogations of witnesses", "note": "The commentary's Yusuf and news examples turn on whether a report is credible; the Mishnah specifies interrogations for witnesses in capital cases. It makes verification a formal procedure rather than an intuition."}

