{"connection_id": "BC-16dff7147bbe396eeeb4", "ref": "WLC:Deut.19.18", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "investigate witness carefully", "note": "The commentary cites the order to research a suspicious report; Israel's judges must investigate thoroughly before deciding whether a witness has lied."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d9b6237f92a9b21bc6f5", "ref": "WLC:Deut.29.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sights without understanding", "note": "The commentary distinguishes noticing from understanding; Moses says Israel still lacks a heart to know, eyes to see, and ears to hear after witnessing signs."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f2f4ea50d491fbd9ffb9", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.10", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "pupil of the eye", "note": "The commentary mentions the figure in the dark of the eye; Deuteronomy says God guards Jacob as the pupil of his eye, making that focal point an image of attentive care."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e7da1c9ad9be808048c7", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary relates faith and haqq to truth and rightness; Moses calls God faithful, without injustice, righteous and upright, joining reliable truth with justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7b25ae3adc2cbb694794", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ح ق ق / חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's truth is something discerned and acted on; Israel must keep and do the prescribed statutes so nations see its wisdom, showing the Hebrew root's legal sense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1d457413190a46ff1dd8", "ref": "WLC:Deut.4.9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes that saw, teaching descendants", "note": "The commentary describes people who see and pass confirmed truth to one another; Deuteronomy tells Israel not to forget what their eyes saw and to teach it to children and grandchildren. Sight becomes communal transmission across generations."}
{"connection_id": "BC-947f51748545865bc23e", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.24", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ح ق ق / חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary joins what is right to deeds that escape loss; the commanded statutes are to be done for Israel's good and preservation of life, showing Hebrew prescribed order rather than mere factual verification."}
{"connection_id": "BC-daf038a3661034e49b77", "ref": "WLC:Deut.6.25", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "doing commandments as righteousness", "note": "The commentary's exception joins belief to righteous deeds; Deuteronomy says doing the commandments carefully will be righteousness for Israel. This gives a Torah formulation in which right standing is tied to practice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1f4f3fb5fafc396d9ee5", "ref": "WLC:Deut.7.9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary explains iman as a person's confirming assent; Deuteronomy calls God נאמן, faithful, as covenant keeper to those who love and obey him. The Hebrew root shifts the emphasis from human assent to divine reliability."}
{"connection_id": "BC-71c108407a097d39f196", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary sets human loss beside deeds; Qoheleth asks what gain a human has from all his toil under the sun, using the corresponding labor root to sharpen the gain-loss question."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db2dfacb71fc0c3fd899", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels righteous deeds with ع م ل and calls the mind's exercise work; Ecclesiastes uses עמל for toil and concludes that the labor brings no gain. The possible root link sets purposeful action against futile toil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3eab5313f20252d20edf", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.11", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary puts good works against loss; Qoheleth surveys all the work his hands have done and the toil spent and finds no lasting gain under the sun."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6f38db1b5991b60c7355", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.22", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary says a person works the mind before certainty; Qoheleth asks what remains from toil and the striving of the heart under the sun."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a9fe09dd5dd9ff105c26", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.4.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary values righteous work; Qoheleth observes that much toil and skill spring from one person's envy of another, questioning work's moral quality."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fb02dcbe324c767db67e", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.8.17", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ع م ل / עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's inquiry culminates in certainty; Qoheleth says a human may toil to search God's work and still fail to find it, setting a concrete limit on investigative labor."}
{"connection_id": "BC-225c53514e3df8d2d181", "ref": "WLC:Exod.14.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing followed by belief", "note": "The commentary says the sequence may be read as seeing and then confirming, though the narrative alone does not require it; Israel sees the great act at the sea, fears the LORD, and believes in him and Moses. This is an explicit biblical sequence of sight and belief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3ea993ee6f40d0094d03", "ref": "WLC:Exod.14.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The source commentary glosses iman as tasdiq; the Hebrew verb for Israel's belief after seeing the sea uses אמנ. The table's proposed cognate connects the lexical root to a scene of evidence and assent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4a2b942cb06bd57f202a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.14.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary lets visible evidence lead to faith; Israel sees the Lord's work at the sea, fears him, and believes him and Moses."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0e7eba979767f9a7ecb6", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hands held steady together", "note": "The commentary ends with people urging one another to patience; Aaron and Hur hold up Moses' hands until sunset so he can persist. The scene gives collective support a concrete role in endurance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fc41650255730f95e52c", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary pairs iman with patience; Exodus uses אמונה for Moses' hands remaining steady while Aaron and Hur support him. This root use emphasizes firmness and endurance rather than assent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb39d749a7665b0e419e", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sustaining another's endurance", "note": "The commentary's final injunction is reciprocal counsel in patience; Aaron and Hur each support Moses so his hands stay raised through the battle. It depicts endurance sustained through another's help."}
{"connection_id": "BC-eb52ef0a5c13ac2af5da", "ref": "WLC:Exod.17.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's faith and patience accompany Moses; his hands remain steady during the battle, illustrating the Hebrew root's physical firmness rather than verbal assent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b2484a31f95721674047", "ref": "WLC:Exod.19.18", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mountain fire", "note": "The commentary notes fire seen from the mountain's side; Sinai is wrapped in smoke because the Lord descends on it in fire, another mountain-fire setting distinct from the bush."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f03008b389b243554772", "ref": "WLC:Exod.2.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "distant watch to know", "note": "The commentary's observer watches from afar to find out what is happening; Moses' sister stands at a distance to learn what will be done to him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95ffcd6076a21ffd0d3a", "ref": "WLC:Exod.20.15", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing sound at Sinai", "note": "The commentary couples seeing a fire with hearing a divine voice; the people at Sinai see thunder and flashes, hear the trumpet, and stand far off."}
{"connection_id": "BC-50d8950c87779815d8c0", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false report and witness", "note": "The commentary contrasts truthful counsel with a report that needs checking; Exodus forbids carrying a false report or joining a malicious witness. The law guards the circulation of claims among a community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b38373f29e73e214f315", "ref": "WLC:Exod.23.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "false report must not be carried", "note": "The commentary ends with recommending verified truth to others; Israel is forbidden to spread a false report or aid a malicious witness, making transmission itself an ethical act."}
{"connection_id": "BC-fd22e040869f57c7f3c1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.2", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses sees fire", "note": "The commentary quotes Musa saying he saw a fire and may bring back a coal or find guidance; Exodus has Moses see the angel of the LORD in a flame within a bush. The shared encounter presents fire as a visible sign, while the Qur'anic gloss stresses wayfinding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e9c03bec2316d69e43cc", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.2", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Moses notices fire", "note": "The commentary's Moses sees a fire on his journey; Exodus locates the divine encounter in a bush burning without being consumed, specifying what caught his eye."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-ae871f0356ed47606413", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "turning aside to inspect", "note": "The commentary develops seeing as noticing and examining; Moses turns aside to see why the bush is not consumed. The biblical scene makes looking an inquiry rather than mere sight."}
{"connection_id": "BC-061aee4c315278d13a9b", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "turn aside to inspect", "note": "Moses' sight of fire becomes investigation in the commentary; here he turns aside to see why the bush is not burned up."}
{"connection_id": "BC-883742abfe7ad5d4cb9f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "a voice from the fire", "note": "The commentary cites Musa's sight of fire and notes that the root's range includes hearing; in Exodus, the LORD calls Moses from the bush and Moses answers. This continues the same encounter through an auditory summons."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8a55a33f6196ab381f2f", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "sight followed by voice", "note": "The commentary joins noticing with hearing; God sees Moses turn to inspect and calls to him from the bush."}
{"connection_id": "BC-415fcd31bcc71e252257", "ref": "WLC:Exod.3.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fear limits sight at fire", "note": "The commentary presents seeing the fire as a path toward certainty; Moses hides his face because he is afraid to look at God, marking a boundary to sight in the same scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1ff51beb2e43aa32eafb", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Moses anticipates disbelief", "note": "The commentary's Yusuf example makes credibility the issue; Moses tells the LORD that Israel will not believe him or listen to his voice. This is a distinct biblical scene where a messenger worries that his report will not be accepted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0847e8ae8a3758c06fc2", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary asks how a heard message is credited; Moses worries Israel will not believe him or listen to his voice when he reports the divine appearance."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5cced7a69e188cd7da04", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "hearing, believing, and worshipping", "note": "The commentary links perception, confirmation, and action; Israel hears that the LORD has visited them, believes, and bows in worship. This gives the sensory-to-assent sequence a communal outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a23ed888a031850917cd", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels iman from ء م ن; Exodus uses the Hebrew אמנ root when Israel believes after hearing that God has visited them. The proposed correspondence links the root to the heard report."}
{"connection_id": "BC-adee011ce6d98eb71e05", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary moves from transmitted words and seen evidence to faith; Israel hears that the Lord has visited them, sees Moses' signs, believes, and bows in response."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a8a636639b9f92f1654d", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.5", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "signs intended to elicit belief", "note": "The commentary glosses iman as tasdiq; after giving Moses signs, the LORD says they are meant so Israel may believe that the God of their ancestors appeared to him. The signs explicitly connect visible evidence with assent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a0accb0c28fdd2959783", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.5", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמן; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels iman as tasdiq; Exodus uses the Hebrew אמן root for Israel's believing in Moses' report after signs. The proposed root correspondence adds a lexical link to the scene's evidence-based belief."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3d1f9c3fc0e726193ac1", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.5", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's assent follows signs that can be tested; God gives Moses a sign so Israel may believe the ancestral God appeared to him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9315d066ed72e16b5544", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "first and second signs", "note": "The commentary connects seeing with becoming sure; Exodus distinguishes a sign that may fail to persuade from a second sign that may be believed. It gives a staged test of a messenger's claim."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b7c0536fdb0da5ebdd10", "ref": "WLC:Exod.4.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary makes belief follow examination of what is perceived; God says that if the people do not believe or heed the first sign, they may believe the second, treating signs as successive grounds for assent."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e0b9ce436cd19dd42035", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.12.2", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing and hearing without response", "note": "The commentary says the surah does not itself state that humanity is blind; Ezekiel calls Israel a rebellious house with eyes to see but not seeing and ears to hear but not hearing. The text names the interpretive condition directly."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a48f43b83cf113f907a1", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.33.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watchman sees and warns", "note": "The commentary has the far-seeing observer transmit what he has established; Ezekiel's watchman sees a sword coming and sounds a warning to the people."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef9cf831a493b9d28df5", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.33.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "failure to pass on what is seen", "note": "The commentary's seeing culminates in recommending truth; if Ezekiel's watchman sees the sword and gives no warning, the endangered person's blood is required of him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f30ab818927f149ee17", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "belief reckoned as righteousness", "note": "The commentary joins iman and haqq as the soul's assent to truth; Genesis says Abram believed YHWH and it was reckoned to him as righteousness. The verse links belief with right standing in a concrete promise scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea5374015ff89093af22", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The source commentary explicitly glosses iman as tasdiq; Genesis uses the Hebrew אמנ root for Abram's belief. The proposed cognate connects the term to a belief that is counted as righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-421d82eef801e3fe4d02", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary defines faith as affirming truth; Abram trusts the Lord's promise and it is reckoned to him as righteousness, linking assent to right standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b170e9552bbb2b3b52c5", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "action discloses allegiance", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with righteous action; after Abraham does not withhold Isaac, the angel says that now he knows Abraham fears God. The test makes conduct disclose the allegiance professed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-436173b0d0650fc94f46", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing a distant place", "note": "The commentary describes the far-seeing eye as watching from a distance; Genesis says Abraham lifts his eyes and sees the place from afar. This is a concrete biblical image of distant sight preceding action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c25cdabd6ff6c3919720", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "goat blood prepared as evidence", "note": "The commentary's Jacob receives a bloodied object with a false story; Genesis says the brothers slaughter a goat and dip Joseph's tunic in its blood before sending it, identifying the evidence as manufactured."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9325a252654e836fb35c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.32", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Yusuf brothers' bloodied robe; visual testimony", "note": "The commentary quotes the brothers anticipating that Yaqub will not believe them; Genesis has them present Joseph's bloodied robe and ask their father to identify it. Both stage a claim through an object offered for inspection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02875c3e7fdd05114526", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.32", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tunic sent for recognition", "note": "The commentary's report to Jacob asks for belief in the brothers' account; Genesis has the brothers send the stained tunic and ask him to recognize whether it is Joseph's."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8d387a69f6328fc65ff", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.33", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jacob's inference from the robe", "note": "In the commentary's Qur'anic scene Yaqub rejects the brothers' account as something their souls invented; Genesis has him infer from the robe that Joseph was devoured. The changed response to misleading evidence is the point."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baf61ca422b5e0a062e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.33", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "bloodied tunic judged", "note": "The commentary says Jacob rejects the sons' story despite the bloody evidence; Genesis has him recognize the tunic and conclude a beast devoured Joseph, making his response to the evidence different."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd24bfd8dea372840c1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.34", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "grief and sabr jamil", "note": "Genesis says Jacob mourns Joseph for many days; the commentary quotes the Qur'anic answer, beautiful patience. The two tellings give distinct responses to the same reported loss without making Genesis deny patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16901c2c69d1392dbd4a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.35", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jacob's response to loss", "note": "The commentary quotes Jacob's beautiful patience after rejecting the report; Genesis depicts his refusal to be comforted and expectation of mourning down to Sheol."}

