{"connection_id": "BC-ef9cf831a493b9d28df5", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.33.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "failure to pass on what is seen", "note": "The commentary's seeing culminates in recommending truth; if Ezekiel's watchman sees the sword and gives no warning, the endangered person's blood is required of him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f30ab818927f149ee17", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "belief reckoned as righteousness", "note": "The commentary joins iman and haqq as the soul's assent to truth; Genesis says Abram believed YHWH and it was reckoned to him as righteousness. The verse links belief with right standing in a concrete promise scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ea5374015ff89093af22", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The source commentary explicitly glosses iman as tasdiq; Genesis uses the Hebrew אמנ root for Abram's belief. The proposed cognate connects the term to a belief that is counted as righteousness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-421d82eef801e3fe4d02", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.6", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary defines faith as affirming truth; Abram trusts the Lord's promise and it is reckoned to him as righteousness, linking assent to right standing."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b170e9552bbb2b3b52c5", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.12", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "action discloses allegiance", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with righteous action; after Abraham does not withhold Isaac, the angel says that now he knows Abraham fears God. The test makes conduct disclose the allegiance professed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-436173b0d0650fc94f46", "ref": "WLC:Gen.22.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing a distant place", "note": "The commentary describes the far-seeing eye as watching from a distance; Genesis says Abraham lifts his eyes and sees the place from afar. This is a concrete biblical image of distant sight preceding action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c25cdabd6ff6c3919720", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.31", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "goat blood prepared as evidence", "note": "The commentary's Jacob receives a bloodied object with a false story; Genesis says the brothers slaughter a goat and dip Joseph's tunic in its blood before sending it, identifying the evidence as manufactured."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9325a252654e836fb35c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.32", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Yusuf brothers' bloodied robe; visual testimony", "note": "The commentary quotes the brothers anticipating that Yaqub will not believe them; Genesis has them present Joseph's bloodied robe and ask their father to identify it. Both stage a claim through an object offered for inspection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-02875c3e7fdd05114526", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.32", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "tunic sent for recognition", "note": "The commentary's report to Jacob asks for belief in the brothers' account; Genesis has the brothers send the stained tunic and ask him to recognize whether it is Joseph's."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8d387a69f6328fc65ff", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.33", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jacob's inference from the robe", "note": "In the commentary's Qur'anic scene Yaqub rejects the brothers' account as something their souls invented; Genesis has him infer from the robe that Joseph was devoured. The changed response to misleading evidence is the point."}
{"connection_id": "BC-baf61ca422b5e0a062e7", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.33", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "bloodied tunic judged", "note": "The commentary says Jacob rejects the sons' story despite the bloody evidence; Genesis has him recognize the tunic and conclude a beast devoured Joseph, making his response to the evidence different."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ccd24bfd8dea372840c1", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.34", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "grief and sabr jamil", "note": "Genesis says Jacob mourns Joseph for many days; the commentary quotes the Qur'anic answer, beautiful patience. The two tellings give distinct responses to the same reported loss without making Genesis deny patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-16901c2c69d1392dbd4a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.37.35", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Jacob's response to loss", "note": "The commentary quotes Jacob's beautiful patience after rejecting the report; Genesis depicts his refusal to be comforted and expectation of mourning down to Sheol."}

