{"connection_id": "BC-aaf59804916433516186", "ref": "WLC:Gen.42.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "test whether report is true", "note": "The commentary treats brothers' words as testable; Joseph detains them and sends one to fetch Benjamin so their statements can be tested."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b8a982e6183913d36563", "ref": "WLC:Gen.42.20", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words verified by a required check", "note": "The commentary quotes confirmation of a saying; Joseph makes the brothers bring Benjamin so their claim can be tested and they will not die. This is a narrative case where corroboration is demanded before a report is accepted."}
{"connection_id": "BC-784f2a0d04dc631ce7ec", "ref": "WLC:Gen.42.20", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary glosses haqq as confirming a saying; Joseph tells his brothers their words will be verified if they bring Benjamin. Genesis uses the Hebrew אמנ root for words made reliable by a check."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a1de84a0ec3b46e74201", "ref": "WLC:Gen.42.20", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "test brothers' words", "note": "The commentary's faith involves testing a report; Joseph requires Benjamin's arrival so the brothers' words may be verified in this family story."}
{"connection_id": "BC-514a5a5c4e2cb821217d", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.26", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "paralel", "basis": "Jacob disbelieves the true report", "note": "The commentary quotes the brothers saying their father would not believe them even if truthful; in Genesis they report Joseph is alive and Jacob does not believe them. The shared family scene turns on whether a report is credible."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5e82776af240f8ee2988", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.26", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary labels iman as confirmation; Genesis uses the Hebrew אמנ root when Jacob does not believe the brothers' true report. The possible cognate appears in the same Yusuf family scene but with truthful news."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9f33f12d060c2c2391d2", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.26", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "unbelieved report of Joseph", "note": "The commentary treats a report as requiring confirmation; Jacob's heart goes numb when told Joseph lives, for he does not believe the speakers."}
{"connection_id": "BC-6fc7273b6fdee3eaf9af", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.27", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wagons as visible corroboration", "note": "The commentary permits a reading in which seeing precedes confirmation; Genesis says Jacob's spirit revives when he sees the wagons Joseph sent. Here visible evidence changes his response to the report."}
{"connection_id": "BC-028d82633da99b38ee2a", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.27", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "words checked by wagons", "note": "The commentary moves from hearing a claim to seeing grounds for certainty; Jacob hears Joseph's words and sees the wagons, then his spirit revives."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8647ef24ea3ce3c1e64c", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.28", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "affirmation after evidence", "note": "The commentary links seeing with confirming what one has been told; after the wagons arrive, Jacob declares that Joseph is alive and says he will go see him. The verse gives the explicit affirmation following the evidence."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e743262ccc02aec839fb", "ref": "WLC:Gen.45.28", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Jacob seeks his own sight", "note": "The commentary's Jacob moves from a doubtful report toward confirmation; after the wagons revive him, he says Joseph is alive and resolves to go see him before he dies."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b64f667cd331bfd7f599", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watching to discern a word", "note": "The commentary joins attentive sight to mental work and a true message; the prophet stations himself on watch to see what God will say and how he should answer."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4fd80c40a9bf03c1dbfe", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.2", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "record seen message", "note": "The commentary's truth is communicated after attentive sight; the prophet must write the vision plainly so its reader may carry the message onward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2a01963cc40bae757a99", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wait for the vision", "note": "The commentary ends its sequence with mutual counsel in patience; Habakkuk says the vision has an appointed time and, even if it seems slow, one must wait for it. Patience is tied to a claim whose fulfillment is awaited."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c79aa6643d2e802981b9", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.3", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wait for truth to arrive", "note": "The commentary pairs verified truth with patience; Habakkuk says the vision awaits its appointed time and must be waited for even if it seems delayed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-725ba194eef79da130da", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteousness and faithfulness", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with righteous deeds; Habakkuk says the righteous live by faithfulness. The verse places righteousness and emunah together while leaving open whether the emphasis is trust or fidelity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cfd4cd99a4b2791f45a0", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary defines iman as assent to truth; Habakkuk says the righteous live by אמונה, often rendered faithfulness. The possible cognate preserves a difference between assent and steadfast fidelity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5accd01d895aa0df00f3", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.4", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary joins faith to patient endurance; amid an awaited vision the righteous lives by faithfulness, expressed by the corresponding Hebrew root."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f0c6abd30245676f014d", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ح ق ق / חקק; table sound correspondence, unverified", "note": "The commentary's haqq means truth, conformity, and confirmed rightness; Isaiah condemns people who decree חקק decrees of injustice. The proposed root correspondence warns that something prescribed can still be false or unjust."}
{"connection_id": "BC-39eff75b234cca8faf50", "ref": "WLC:Isa.10.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "ح ق ق / חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's haqq is fitting, verified truth; Isaiah condemns those who inscribe unjust decrees, showing that a formally fixed rule can contradict justice."}
{"connection_id": "BC-4f1a6ef5c00450f21734", "ref": "WLC:Isa.21.7", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watch and listen closely", "note": "The commentary's observer sees at a distance and hears; Isaiah instructs a watcher who sees riders to listen with close attention."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2d0a3cbe8b780f5e5114", "ref": "WLC:Isa.21.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "patient watch", "note": "The commentary adds endurance to distant observation; Isaiah's watchman reports standing continually by day and through the nights at his post."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e6c14bec2976c3affdc6", "ref": "WLC:Isa.21.9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "observed news declared", "note": "The commentary ends in passing verified information on; the watchman reports the arriving riders and announces Babylon's fall."}
{"connection_id": "BC-dce8c0564d5d86a7cf51", "ref": "WLC:Isa.24.5", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ح ق ق / חקק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary joins truth and right conduct against loss; Isaiah says inhabitants pollute the earth by transgressing laws and violating an ordinance, showing the Hebrew root in a communal failure to keep prescribed order."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cab2b19f3760b3c59bba", "ref": "WLC:Isa.28.16", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary pairs belief with patience; Isaiah says the one trusting the tested cornerstone will not act in haste, linking assent with steadiness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bfbf22f8b9d52005f30c", "ref": "WLC:Isa.43.10", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary's witnesses confirm what is real; Israel is called God's witness so they may know, believe, and understand his identity."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0f4536f2b92186bc02a3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.52.8", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "watchmen see and voice together", "note": "The commentary's far-seeing eyes lead to shared transmission; Zion's watchmen lift their voices together because they see the Lord returning eye to eye."}
{"connection_id": "BC-787d337fe22bfe7ff79a", "ref": "WLC:Isa.53.1", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "ء م ن / אמנ; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary defines belief as crediting a communicated truth; Isaiah asks who has believed the speakers' report and to whom the Lord's arm has been revealed, pairing received testimony with disclosure."}
{"connection_id": "BC-24dbee00d9fbbe8ae0c5", "ref": "WLC:Isa.6.10", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes and ears shut", "note": "The commentary leaves blindness as an unproven reading; Isaiah describes dull hearts, heavy ears, and shut eyes, with turning and healing as the alternative. The passage makes sensory incapacity a judgment rather than a fact implied by loss alone."}
{"connection_id": "BC-56a1e02e89a19388faf3", "ref": "WLC:Isa.6.9", "target": "sec8", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "seeing without perceiving", "note": "The commentary says loss need not mean blindness and the exception need not mean sight; Isaiah is explicitly told that people will hear without understanding and see without perceiving. This gives the proposed sensory reading an explicit prophetic parallel."}

