+{"connection_id":"BC-27879fb7a2a27dba1366","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.28.17","status":"rejected","reason":"The disciples see yet some doubt; this repeats the nonautomatic sight-belief relation stated more fully in John 20:25-29.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.28.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-702e875c897ced64acb5","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.7.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Fruit identifies false prophets, but the test is conduct over time, not seeing a claimed event or checking a report.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.7.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-73b47d54208a465392ff","ref":"SBLGNT:Matt.7.16","status":"rejected","reason":"Knowing prophets by fruits is a valid conduct test but not the information-testing process in the quoted Yusuf and news scenes.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Matt.7.16"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-dff0d33f52633c4d5b85","ref":"SBLGNT:Rev.1.11","status":"rejected","reason":"John is told to write his vision to seven churches; this is commissioned revelation, not evidence evaluation before acceptance.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.1.11"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-652961146ba31e828794","ref":"SBLGNT:Rev.3.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Laodicea's blindness is an image for spiritual poverty despite a wealth claim, not the lexical meaning of insan or khusr.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.3.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-f17e0eb5b1e63df54a5c","ref":"SBLGNT:Rev.3.18","status":"rejected","reason":"Eye salve is part of Laodicea's metaphorical remedy; it does not describe the ordinary act of verifying a report.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rev.3.18"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-3a6220bd6150f80f9c0f","ref":"SBLGNT:Rom.10.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Faith from hearing Christ's proclamation is a theological route to belief; 1 John 1:1-3 better includes seeing, hearing and communication together.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rom.10.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-b6138f7f39b6d03a9e71","ref":"SBLGNT:Rom.10.17","status":"rejected","reason":"Hearing the preached word produces faith but does not require checking the word; the kept note uses the fuller witness chain.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rom.10.17"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-fdaa43b6f0a9c8ad0c06","ref":"SBLGNT:Rom.2.13","status":"rejected","reason":"Hearers versus doers of law concerns justification by obedience, not factual corroboration after hearing.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rom.2.13"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-7b664f35cd4a546e2ecc","ref":"SBLGNT:Rom.4.3","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul quotes Gen 15:6 for credited righteousness; ¶24's Hebrew cognate note uses the earlier verse without imposing Paul's reading.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rom.4.3"],"annotations":[]}
+{"connection_id":"BC-1ba9d9960d98403bcae1","ref":"SBLGNT:Rom.4.5","status":"rejected","reason":"Paul contrasts wage earning with credited faith, a Christian justification argument distinct from ¶24's sensory confirmation.","paragraphs":[],"evidence":["SBLGNT:Rom.4.5"],"annotations":[]}
