{"root":"ء م ن","decision":"used","hebrew":["אמנ"],"reason":"Hebrew and Biblical Aramaic אמנ ranges from trusting a report to firmness and fidelity; Isa 7:9 and 2 Chr 20:20 play on believing and being established. The Arabic link is sound correspondence only, not a borrowing claim.","paragraphs":[24],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-002"]}
{"root":"ء ن س","decision":"used","hebrew":["אנש"],"reason":"BDB cites an Arabic cognate for Hebrew אנש; enosh denotes mortal humanity in Ps 8:5 and Ps 103:15, while Aramaic enash names a humanlike figure in Dan 7:13. Hebrew אנס instead means compel or oppress and does not support the seeing gloss.","paragraphs":[24],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-001"]}
{"root":"ح ق ق","decision":"used","hebrew":["חקק"],"reason":"Only a sound correspondence is available; Hebrew חקק means inscribe or prescribe, not factual truth. Isa 10:1 condemns unjust decrees, making that semantic boundary useful here.","paragraphs":[24],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003"]}
{"root":"ع م ل","decision":"used","hebrew":["עמל"],"reason":"Only a sound correspondence is available; Hebrew עמל denotes toil, trouble and sometimes investigative effort (Ps 73:16), and can describe oppressive labor (Isa 10:1). This range partly overlaps mental work but not righteous deeds as such.","paragraphs":[24],"annotations":["S103-TEV-SYD-003","S103-TEV-SYD-004"]}

